= Related Works <relatedworks>
pygrametl introduced a programming based ETL framework which could achieve competitive execution perforamnce compared with established graphical ETL tools @pygrametl. In the evaluated scenario, pygrametl performed similarly to Pentaho Data Integration in terms of execution time, while consuming less CPU time and memory. It also appeared to scale better with increasing dataset sizes.

Subsequently, parallel execution was introduced through multiprocessing in CPython and multithreading in Jython, allowing ETL tasks and data partitions to be processed concurrently @pygrametlPara. The evaluation demonstrated substantial performance gains over sequential execution, with reported improvements ranging from 130% to 183% across datasets containing 5 to 100 million facts. These gains came at the cost of a 53–55% increase in CPU time. The parallel implementation utilized available CPU resources more intensively, allowing ETL processes to complete in considerably less wall-clock time. The results demonstrate the potential of parallel execution to significantly reduce ETL processing time without a proportional increase in CPU consumption.
